<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Crevillent</h1><p class="st-meta">1243 CE – 1318 CE (approx.) · capital Crevillent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Muslim lordship near Elche whose ra'is ruled under Christian overlords for some 75 years after the fall of Murcia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1243, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-taifa-of-murcia">Third Taifa of Murcia</a> accepted Castilian overlordship by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcaraz">Treaty of Alcaraz</a>, the Banu Hudayr family kept control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crevillent">Crevillent</a> and its district as autonomous Muslim lords, or ra'is.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known only from scattered documents, the lordship was a Muslim enclave of irrigated farmland under Christian protection, first of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile</a> and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-torrellas">Treaty of Torrellas</a> of 1304, of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a>. It survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar-revolt-of-1264-1266">Mudéjar revolt</a> of 1264-1266 and kept contacts with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1318 the last ra'is left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crevillent">Crevillent</a> was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-valencia">Kingdom of Valencia</a>. Its Muslim population remained as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjares</a> until the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> in 1609.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1243 CE – 1318 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.44%;width:1.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1243 CE – 1318 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Crevillent</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crevillente, Banu Hudayr</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1280&amp;focus=taifa-of-crevillent" aria-label="Taifa of Crevillent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4660 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Crevillent lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4980 1060L4885 950M4980 1060L4984 945"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4885 950h0M4984 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4980 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4980 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Crevillent. <a href="/atlas?year=1280&amp;focus=taifa-of-crevillent">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

